\paragraph{Empirical profile.}
On SciERC, grammar constraints eliminate relation vocabulary violations entirely (1.39\%\,$\to$\,0\%) and entity type violations entirely (0.40\%\,$\to$\,0\%), with no measurable F1 impact.
Domain/range violations remain effectively unchanged (15.37\%\,$\to$\,14.84\%). This confirms that grammar constraints enforce structural but not semantic validity.


\subsection{Semantic Layer}

\subsubsection{Position Detection}
\label{sec:position-detection}

At each step $t$, the processor determines which field is currently being generated by pattern-matching on the decoded prefix.
We define five field positions (\textsc{EntityType}, \textsc{RelationType}, \textsc{HeadType}, \textsc{TailType}, and \textsc{FreeText}), identified via JSON key patterns (e.g., tokens following \texttt{"head\_type":} indicate \textsc{HeadType}).
Semantic masks activate only at type-bearing positions (\textsc{HeadType}, \textsc{TailType}).

tool_args.new_string:
\subsubsection{Position Detection}
\label{sec:position-detection}

At each step $t$, the processor pattern-matches the decoded prefix to identify the current field position; semantic masks activate only at type-bearing positions (\textsc{HeadType}, \textsc{TailType}).

\subsubsection{Grammar-Semantic Interaction}
\label{sec:interaction}

The final mask is the conjunction of $G(t)$ and $S(t)$, permitting only tokens satisfying both layers.
This embodies an \emph{enabling dependency}: semantic lookup requires relation types to be valid members of $\mathcal{R}$, guaranteed only by the grammar layer; without it, semantic-only constraints reduce DR by only 24\%.
When $S(t) \cap G(t) = \emptyset$, the system falls back to grammar-only control (\emph{grammar-first fallback}), preserving structural validity at the cost of a potential type violation.
Formal analysis of composition, fallback behavior, and constraint tightness is in \S\ref{sec:composition}.

\paragraph{S2: Two-pass decoding.}\label{sec:s2}
A first pass extracts entities under grammar constraints; extracted entities compile an input-dependent grammar for the second pass, which generates relations under both grammar and semantic constraints.
S1 achieves higher R-F1 at comparable DR (\S\ref{sec:scierc-results}) and is the primary configuration.

\paragraph{Composability with fine-tuning.}
Fine-tuning narrows the fallback regime: DR drops from 0.08--10.21\% (zero-shot) to $\leq$\,0.20\% (LoRA), and CF from 2.57--12.02\% to $\leq$\,1.18\%, while R-F1 remains within $\pm$0.6\,pp (\S\ref{sec:composability}).
Under fine-tuning, the grammar layer becomes a no-op and the semantic layer alone reduces residual DR by 39--72\%.

